    {
      "id": "1549920014507712643",
      "type": "Default",
      "timestamp": "2026-09-16T23:08:47.58+00:00",
      "timestampEdited": null,
      "callEndedTimestamp": null,
      "isPinned": false,
      "content": "Also remember DDR2 (e.g.) RAM is a bus thing; you're not prevented from addressing an IO controller over the bus if that IO controller plays by the rules; you're also allowed to hand over responsibility of refresh management and segment off drive of the command/address bus of such memory to a different chip temporarily.\nThough I'd probably not suggest splitting off in a way that forces you to use some external MUX crossbar chip to dynamically route memory bus data wires between different \"memory hosts\".\n\nMemory \"mapped\" IO is probably the most realistic way, though if you own the memory controller logic you'll be VERY free to do things differently for the protocol as long as it fits with the actual memory access pattern structure and the normal memory chips don't get confused from what you're doing to them.",
      "author": {
        "id": "262706220073222144",
        "name": "namibj",
        "discriminator": "0000",
        "nickname": "namibj",
        "color": null,
        "isBot": false,
        "roles": [],
        "avatarUrl": "2026-09_media/f885abc0dddd0a33e4d4991b3c99091d-7ACD2.png"
      },
